This is a repository to test single layer Compton cameras with pixel detector such as Timepix3

Code to simulate compton cameras differs according to Gate version:
- First module dedicated to compton cameras appeared in Gate 9.2 (see CCMod paper)
- Gate 9.3 introduced a new way to handle digitizers
- Gate 10 completely changed, scripts are now in python and compton camera actors have not been re-implemented (yet?)

Bad cones can originate from:
- Doppler broadening => physics list emlivermore
- X-ray fluorescence => cuts < 100 um
- Limited detector resolution in:
  - energy (threshold and ToT fluctuations)
  - position (pixel segmentation, charge sharing, etc)
  - time (pile-up in coincident events)
- ???

Hit root files have different column names between Gate 9 and 10... Fluorescence events can be identified with:
- Gate 10: a gamma row with emission line energy in column 'TotalEnergyDeposit'.
 EventID  TrackID  ParentID  ParentParticleName  ParticleName  KineticEnergy  TotalEnergyDeposit  TrackCreatorProcess  ProcessDefinedStep
       8        1         0             unknown         gamma        140.000              11.107                 none                none
  => There's only one row for both the parent X-ray and the subsequently emitted fluorescent X-ray... the only indication
     that it was a photoelectric interaction is the TrackCreatorProcess of subsequent track ID.
- Gate 9.2: a gamma row (PDGEncoding = 22) with emission line energy in column 'energyIniT'. It usually follows a row with a photoelectric event:
 eventID  PDGEncoding  trackID  parentID     edep  energyFinal  energyIniT  postStepProcess  processName  layerName
     316           22        1         0  0.12500      0.00000   140.00000             phot                absorber
     316           22        3         1  1.40230      0.00000    10.94200             phot         phot   absorber
NOTES:
 * in Gate 10, the emitted fluorescent X-ray does not seem to be transported at all...
 * the example above used emlivermore with 0.1 um cuts and Germanium (K lines 9.89 keV and 10.98 keV). The difference
   (11.107 vs 10.942 keV) might be due to different geant4 verions / physics lists?